<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inner Mongolia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> is the southern part of the Mongolian plateau, lying along the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, now an autonomous region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. Its grasslands were the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghu">Donghu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and Wuhuan and the base of states such as Dai. Under the Qing its Mongol banners were governed separately from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outer-mongolia">Outer Mongolia</a>, and in the 1930s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> sponsored the Mengjiang regime there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia.jpg" alt="Inner Mongolia" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ScareCriterion12 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%88%90%E5%90%89%E6%80%9D%E6%B1%97%E9%99%B52021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> is a long, narrow region along the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It borders eight provincial-level divisions and shares two-thirds of the length of China's border with the country of Mongolia, as well as a small section with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Because it stretches so far from east to west, its eastern part is often counted with Northeastern China and its western part with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a>. The winters are very long, cold and dry, the springs arid with large sandstorms, and the summers warm to hot. The terms Inner and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outer-mongolia">Outer Mongolia</a> in Chinese derive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> words dorgi and tulergi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in the 13th century, the central and western parts, especially the Hetao region, passed back and forth between Chinese farming communities in the south and northern peoples including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>, Khitan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> and Tujue. The history of the east is mostly one of alternating Tungusic and Mongol tribes. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> unified the Mongol tribes in 1206, the Western Xia was conquered in 1227 and the Jin dynasty fell in 1234. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> in 1271, and his summer capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Shangdu</a> (Xanadu) stood near present-day Dolonnor.</p><p>After the Ming overthrew the Yuan in 1368, they took parts of the region and rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a> roughly along its present southern edge. After the Tumu incident of 1449, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> ruler Esen taishi captured the Chinese emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> moved south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outer-mongolia">Outer Mongolia</a>, and until 1635 Inner Mongolia was the political and cultural centre of the Mongols of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> gained far-reaching control of its tribes in 1635, when Ligden Khan's son surrendered the Chakhar Mongols, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> administered the Mongolian Plateau differently in each region.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outer-mongolia">Outer Mongolia</a> gained independence from the Qing in 1911, and almost all banners of Inner Mongolia recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a> as supreme ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, though internal strife prevented unification. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> suppressed the Mongol rebellions and reorganised the region into provinces, among them Rehe and Chahar. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Chinese Communists, with Soviet support, established the Inner Mongolia Autonomous Region in 1947. It first covered only the Hulunbuir region and was expanded westwards over the next decade into its present elongated shape under its leader Ulanhu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Inner Mongolia Autonomous Region has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohhot">Hohhot</a> as its capital, with Baotou, Chifeng, Tongliao and Ordos among its other major cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> form the majority of the population, and the region has the largest Mongol population in the world, with over 4 million people. Mongolian is written in the traditional Mongolian script. Herding is the traditional subsistence of the grasslands, and Buddhist temples such as Dazhao, built in 1580, survive in Hohhot.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inner Mongolia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Khitan were a Mongolic-speaking people of the Xar Moron and Liao river basins in what is now eastern <b>Inner Mongolia</b>, organised in a confederation of eight tribes whose leaders were chosen for fixed terms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…former subjects who built a khaganate on the steppe by 402; their raids were constant, and in 423 a wall over 2,000 li (1,080 kilometres) was built from Chicheng in Hebei to Wuyuan in <b>Inner Mongolia</b>, the first Great Wall since the Han, and a second in 446.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xianbei mountains are usually placed in the region of the Greater Khingan range, in eastern <b>Inner Mongolia</b> and western Manchuria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the southwest the religions of the Zhuang, Yi, Yao, Tujia and Miao have been “Taoised”, and in Tibet, <b>Inner Mongolia</b> and western China the cult of Gesar has grown.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>It is the official language of Mongolia, where nearly 3.6 million people spoke it in a 2014 estimate, and of China’s <b>Inner Mongolia</b>, where about 2.1 million speak it.</blockquote><a href="/language/mongolian">Mongolian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahmud al-Kashgari’s Compendium and Yusuf Khass Hajib’s Kutadgu Bilig are honoured as classics of Turkic culture, and the Old Uyghur script lives on in the traditional Mongolian script still used in <b>Inner Mongolia</b> and revived in Mongolia.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
